(*
Issue #203: EP §6.8.7.3's variant-part-value grammar bypassed all four
existing parser depth guards (Parser.h:50-128) too, and in a way the
structured-value-arm cycle in the sibling test here does not reach: a
variant part's field-list recurses straight back into
parseVariantPartValue ('value [case 1 of [case 1 of [...]]]') without
ever passing back through parseComponentValue, so a single shared depth
counter checked only in parseComponentValue would still leave this shape
free to exhaust the real C++ stack.  600 levels is comfortably past the
500-deep ceiling (MaxValueDepth, ParseInit.cpp) while staying fast to
parse here; the actual crash threshold (tens of thousands) is exercised
end-to-end, under the real compiler's real stack, by
test/Driver/ParserRobustness/deeply-nested-structured-value-does-not-crash-the-compiler.pas.

Checked in verbatim rather than generated at build time -- lit has no
loop-emitting primitive, and the source is deterministic; nobody should
have to look at 600 levels of 'case 1 of [' to read this file, so the
lit directives come first.

Unlike the sibling arm-value test, unwinding here falls through into
statement parsing re-interpreting the still-unconsumed 'case' token as
the start of an ordinary case-statement (a different production that
also starts with 'case'), so the diagnostic count is a little larger
than the minimal cascade-free case but still small and bounded,
independent of nesting depth.

RUN: not %plang_ir -std=iso10206 -dump-parse-tree %s 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
RUN: grep -c "error:" %t.err | FileCheck --check-prefix=COUNT --strict-whitespace --match-full-lines %s
*)

(*
CHECK: error: structured value is nested too deeply
COUNT:11
*)

